# Review is required for the things that decide behaviour, and for the front door.
#
# Manifests are here because "no new dependencies without a reason that survives scrutiny"
# is a rule nothing else enforces. deny.toml is here because it is where that rule's
# mechanical half is granted an exception: an ignored advisory, an allowed licence, a
# duplicate version nobody has to account for again.
#
# The rest is what decides what executes with this tree checked out, and who decides it: the
# workflows, the image the released binaries are built in, the script an install runs, the hook npm
# runs on install, and the three lockfiles. None of these was owned, so the one approval the
# protection requires for a change that moves a pinned step onto a movable tag was owed to nobody
# in particular. required-checks.txt is here for the same reason: it is the tree's statement of
# which checks hold a merge.
#
# Last matching pattern wins, so keep the more specific rules below the general ones.
#
# netzenbot-reviewer is the account the scheduled review-prs job reviews as. GitHub requests the
# owners of every file a pull request touches, and only the last matching rule counts, so the
# account is on the catch-all rule and repeated on each rule below it: a change to only one of
# those files would otherwise ask bbondy and never the reviewer. Nothing waits on it; the ruleset
# does not require code-owner review.

* @netzenbot-reviewer

